% Chapter 63 of the tariff (Section XI): other made up textile articles;
% needlecraft sets; worn clothing and worn textile articles; rags — all ten
% headings (6301 to 6310) and their 52 six-digit subheadings. A library: it
% includes chapter_42.le (and through it chapter_64.le, chapter_71.le,
% chapter_39.le, section_xi.le and gri.le), chapter_61.le, chapter_62.le
% and chapter_67.le, because the notes of Chapters 39, 61, 62, 63 and 64
% and of Section XI send goods between these chapters; tariff.le includes
% it.
%
% Text: HTSUS Revision 23 (2026), Chapter 63 notes 1 to 3, subheading note
% 1, and the terms of the headings and subheadings (https://hts.usitc.gov/);
% Section XI note 7 ("made up") and note 2 (the textile material that
% predominates, in section_xi.le); Chapter 39 note 2(p), Chapter 61 note
% 2(b), Chapter 62 note 2(a), Chapter 64 note 1(c). The subheadings are
% the six-digit HS ones. The EU Combined Nomenclature Explanatory Notes (CN
% EN) to Chapter 63 name some articles (beds for animals, camping goods, tea
% towels). US Customs rulings name the articles and show how the headings
% are read.
%
% How the chapter is read. The facts of an article are its kind (a blanket,
% a tablecloth, a curtain, a shoelace...), the fabric and the material:
%   the kind of G is K                    (a kind listed below; its family
%                                          names the heading)
%   G is made up by P                     (Section XI note 7: hemming,
%                                          sewing, knitting to shape...)
%   the fabric construction of G is knitted | woven | nonwoven
%   G contains N percent of M             (the textile material, Section XI
%                                          note 2, section_xi.le)
%   G is of textile material              (when the text gives no fibre)
%   the fabric of G is printed            (bed linen, 6302.21 to 6302.29)
%   the fabric of G is terry              (toilet linen, 6302.60)
%   G is made from warp knit fabric impregnated or coated with an
%       insecticide of subheading note one        (bed nets, 6304.20)
%   G shows signs of appreciable wear; G is entered in bulk or in bales,
%       sacks or similar packings                 (6309, note 3)
%   G is put up in packings for retail sale       (6308)
%   G is sorted                                   (rags, 6310.10)
% Subchapter I (6301 to 6307) takes only made up articles of a textile
% fabric (note 1). A finished article of a kind that the headings name is
% made up: the title of the subchapter names these articles "made up". A
% good of Chapters 56 to 62, and worn articles of 6309, are not of
% subchapter I (note 2). Heading 6307 is the subchapter's residual
% heading: it takes the other made up articles the chapter names, and a
% made up textile article that names no kind. GRI 3(a) prefers the headings
% 6301 to 6306 to it.
%
% The subheadings read the textile material of what the good is classified
% as consisting of (GRI 3(b): a set of sheets takes the material of the
% sheet that gives it its essential character): cotton, wool or fine
% animal hair, synthetic or artificial fibres (Section XI subheading note
% 2), jute and the other bast fibres of heading 5303, strip of polyethylene
% or polypropylene (6305.33), or other.
%
% Outside the model: bedding of heading 9404 (pillows, cushions, quilts,
% sleeping bags: Section XI note 1(s)), toys of Chapter 95 (note 1(t)),
% headgear of Chapter 65 (note 1(o)), carpets of Chapter 57, fabrics of
% Chapters 56 to 60, seat covers of motor vehicles (heading 8708, HQ
% H249319). A claim that a good belongs there is answered by the office
% (gri.le). Fabrics coated with plastics that are of Chapter 39 (Section
% XI note 1(h)) are not told apart.
%
% Not modelled: the lines below six digits (napped, embroidery or lace,
% pile or tufted construction, damask, backpacking tents, labels, cords,
% lacings, surgical drapes, flags, face masks, wall banners).
%
% Provenance: every rule and table is labelled and cites the passage it
% encodes; the documents say where their text is (sources/), so the editor
% shows each passage and the verifier checks every quotation.
%
% DISCLAIMER. This program is an example of Logical English. It is provided
% "as is", without warranty of any kind, express or implied, including any
% warranty that it is accurate, complete, up to date or fit for a particular
% purpose. It is not an official reading of the tariff or of the rulings it
% cites, it is not a binding classification ruling, and it is not legal or
% customs advice. Its answers may be wrong: do not rely on them for a real
% import, export or declaration; consult the official texts, the customs
% authority and a qualified professional. Its authors accept no liability for
% any loss or damage arising from its use.

the target language is: prolog.

the templates are:
    *a family* is a family of chapter sixty-three goods.
    *a family* is a family of subchapter one.
    *a good* is made up by *a process*; undefined.
    *a process* is a process of section eleven note seven.
    *a good* is made up.
    *a good* is of textile material; undefined.
    *a good* is of textile fabric.
    *a good* is a made up textile article; memorable.
    the fabric of *a good* is printed; undefined.
    *a good* is made from warp knit fabric impregnated or coated with an insecticide of subheading note one; undefined.
    *a good* shows signs of appreciable wear; undefined.
    *a good* is entered in bulk or in bales, sacks or similar packings; undefined.
    *a family* is a family of the textile articles of note three.
    *a good* is an article of chapter sixty-three note three.
    *a good* is put up in packings for retail sale; undefined.
    *a good* is sorted; undefined.
    *a material* is a textile bast fibre of heading 5303.
    *a material* is a strip of polyethylene or polypropylene.
    *a good* is of bast fibres of heading 5303.
    *a good* is a bed net of subheading note one.
    the chapter sixty-three material of *a good* is *a class*; memorable.
    the knit class of *a good* is *a class*.
    the print class of *a good* is *a class*.
    the towel fabric of *a good* is *a class*.
    the sack form of *a good* is *a form*.
    the furnishing group of *a good* is *a group*.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the material *a material* is *a code* under table blankets.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for *a group* with knit *a knit* with material *a material* with print *a print* is *a code* under table bed and table linen.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the towel material *a material* with fabric *a fabric* is *a code* under table toilet linen.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the knit *a knit* with curtain material *a material* is *a code* under table curtains.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the furnishing *a group* with knit *a knit* with material *a material* is *a code* under table furnishings.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the sack material *a material* with form *a form* is *a code* under table sacks.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the cover *a group* of material *a material* is *a code* under table tarpaulins and tents.
    the subheading in heading *a heading* for the article *a group* is *a code* under table other made up articles.

